From d1dc959b69249d681a26b0916fa2f91e82d012cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:33:55 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(motion): the plan's crossing check reads a shared end
 a rounding apart as a touch

The adaptive planner's self-crossing check (adaptive/guard.rs) tested
exact signs, so two walks that meet at one vertex reached twice - an
orbit closing a ulp off its start, a microlift return landing on a chord
end, a ring starting on the last ring's vertex - could read as a proper
crossing and send a good level to Offset rings.

The scan now asks `crosses`: the proper-crossing test with "on the
other chord's line" read to within TOUCH = 1e-9 mm instead of an exact
zero. Every contact the old law accepted exactly (shared vertex, T-touch,
collinear retrace) is now accepted a rounding off too; a real crossing
still crosses. A non-finite point reads as a crossing instead of being
dropped by the length filter. `proper` (used by lift_link) is unchanged.

Fixes #1048

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
